## Code Style & Readability

The code around you may be messy. That is not a license to match it: every line you
add, edit or move must be clean, readable and consistently formatted - regardless of
how the surrounding code looks. Never copy the surrounding style when it is bad.

### Formatting (non-negotiable)
- Indentation: consistent 2 spaces. Tabs only if the whole file already uses tabs AND the formatter config demands it.
- One statement per line. Never squeeze multiple statements or a multi-step expression into one line.
- No chained ternaries (`a ? b : c ? d : e`). Use `if/else` or a lookup map. A single short ternary is fine.
- No long `&&` / `||` chains - split into named boolean variables with clear names.
- Braces always, even for one-line `if` bodies. No `if (x) doThing();` on one line.
- Max ~100 chars per line. Wrap long arguments/conditions with one argument per line.
- Blank lines between logical blocks. No 10+ consecutive lines with no blank line.
- Do NOT fight the project formatter when it is configured (prettier/eslint/biome): run it, let it decide final whitespace. But if the formatter is NOT configured, use these rules as the standard.

### Delivery (tool edits)
- Never write compressed one-line blocks, collapsed indentation, or edits that squeeze multiple statements onto one line.
- Every line you add or change must already be properly formatted before you finish — do not rely on a later formatter pass.
- If an edit would be unreadable, split it into smaller blocks instead of shipping a messy patch.

### Structure
- Functions: keep them short and single-purpose. If a function needs a comment to explain its job, it is too long - split it.
- Max ~3 levels of indentation. If you need more, extract a helper function.
- Early return instead of `else` nesting: handle errors/edge cases first, then the happy path.
- Name things for what they do: verbs for functions, nouns for data. No `data`, `temp`, `x`, `obj`, `result` for non-trivial values.
- No copy-paste of blocks longer than ~5 lines: extract a function or generalize.
- No "spaghetti": no goto-style control flow, no deeply nested callbacks, no mutation chains that jump between files. Each function reads top-to-bottom.

### Types (TypeScript)
- Prefer explicit types over `any`. Define interfaces/types for shared shapes instead of repeating inline object literals.
- No type gymnastics: no double casts, no `as unknown as`, no `@ts-ignore` to silence real errors. If the type fights you, the structure is wrong - simplify it.
- Import only what you use. No unused variables, parameters, or imports.

### Changes
- The code YOU touch becomes clean, even if the surrounding code is not. Do not leave your edit looking like the mess around it.
- Format every line you add or change; never reformat unrelated files or unrelated blocks in the same file.
- No dead code, commented-out blocks, or `console.log` leftovers in deliverables.
- Deliver complete, working code: no stubs, TODOs, or "placeholder" branches unless the user asked for them.

### File scope (never touch)
- Never edit `node_modules/`, `dist/`, `build/`, `.astro/`, `.wrangler/`, `.next/`, `.firecrawl/`, generated files, `.git/`, lockfiles, or the REST of `.pi/` outside the wiki: never touch `.pi/cursor-agents.json`, `.pi/pi-block-state.json`, or `.pi/agent/` (provider state, global config with credentials). `.pi/memory/` (the wiki) is yours to edit - register findings there. The `.cursorignore` exclusions are intentional boundaries - do not bypass them with shell/rg.
- Never edit vendor or third-party code, even inside `src/`: files with a license header, "extract from ... source", "Copyright ... Authors", bundled/minified third-party code. Inspect if needed, never modify.

### API error responses
- Clients see safe, generic, or curated text only. Internal detail stays in server logs.
- Backend: never return raw `err.message`, stack traces, SQL/vendor errors, or paths in JSON. Log full detail; respond with fixed or status-based generic strings. `5xx` always generic. `4xx` only literal handler strings, not exception propagation.
- Frontend: never show `err.message` or server `message`/`error` fields in UI. Use a central helper mapping HTTP status to fixed copy with an explicit fallback. Do not prefer server text over status fallbacks for `5xx`.
- When touching error handling: fix the central helper first, then call sites.

### Frontend: ZERO comments (hard rule)
- Any file that ships to the frontend (`.tsx`, `.jsx`, `.vue`, `.svelte`, `.astro`, `.ts`/`.js` imported by UI code, `.css`/`.scss` bundled for the browser, HTML) must contain NO comments at all.
- This includes "simple" or "obvious" comments, section titles, TODO notes, and commented-out code. All of it is forbidden in frontend output.
- Reason: comments in frontend bundles are noise shipped to the client; the project standard is zero comments in anything the browser receives.
- If a frontend rule or decision must be documented, put it in the project wiki (`.pi/memory/`) or a README - never in the frontend source.
- Exception: license headers if the project's build tooling requires them, and nothing else.
